# A week of things to do: a day and an action together in one sentence.
lesson language-my-week v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=2 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "My week"
  goal "Say what you do on each day of the week in [[language.name]]."

  look {
    scene 31x9 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        weatherweek week days=["[[time.monday]]", "[[time.tuesday]]", "[[time.wednesday]]", "[[time.thursday]]", "[[time.friday]]", "[[time.saturday]]", "[[time.sunday]]"] skies=["sun", "cloud", "sun", "rain", "sun", "sun", "sunny-spells"] temps=["", "", "", "", "", "", ""]
      }
    }
    say """
      Ana's week: [[routine.on-monday-i-swim]], on Monday I swim.
      [[routine.on-tuesday-i-play-football]] [[routine.on-wednesday-i-play-the-piano]]
      [[routine.on-thursday-i-read-in-the-library]] [[routine.on-friday-i-go-to-the-cinema]]
      [[routine.on-saturday-i-visit-my-grandmother]] [[routine.on-sunday-i-do-nothing]] Every
      sentence has a day and a thing done.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.my-week-name k=4
    }
    show language.my-week-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.my-week-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.my-week-write k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show language.my-week-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.my-week-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.my-week-write k=4 easy-k=3
    show language.my-week-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.my-week-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.my-week-write k=3
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-my-week-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.my-week-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A day and a thing done: [[routine.on-monday-i-swim]] On Monday I swim."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is the right day with the wrong thing done, or the reverse: every
    naming question offers both mistakes beside the right sentence, so a wrong pick shows which part
    was missed. The two-star question is about how the language puts a day in a sentence. From this
    grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word typed without its accent is not
    taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by the
    device. If the child is stuck, make a week chart at home and say one sentence for each day. Off
    the page, keep a diary of the week in the language, one line a day. It meets England's key stage
    2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "speak in sentences, using familiar
    vocabulary, phrases and basic language structures"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages:
    "Can talk about themselves after organizing the contents they want to communicate by using
    simple words and phrases and basic expressions"; and CEFR A2, "Can communicate in simple and
    routine tasks requiring a simple and direct exchange of information on familiar and routine
    matters". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[routine.on-friday-i-go-to-the-cinema]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[routine.on-monday-i-swim]] and [[routine.on-tuesday-i-play-football]] and the words to write are [[routine.i-read]] and [[routine.i-dance]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[routine.on-saturday-i-visit-my-grandmother]] and [[routine.on-sunday-i-do-nothing]] and the words to write are [[routine.i-go]], [[routine.i-play-an-instrument]] and [[routine.i-dance]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
